<DecisionBrief id="mobility-model" title="The rebalancing decision" class="mobility-brief" items={[{"label": "The problem", "text": "During the morning, some cycle-hire stations record more departures than arrivals; others record the reverse. Before 06:00, where should a limited number of bicycle moves be concentrated to offset that expected imbalance?"}, {"label": "The decision", "text": "Choose a whole number of bikes to add to or take from each eligible station. Every addition must be matched by a removal elsewhere. One bike taken and delivered counts as one move."}, {"label": "The information", "text": "Earlier same-weekday flows estimate each station’s 06:00–10:00 net outflow. The later morning’s recorded departures and arrivals evaluate the plan. Forecasts are allowed to be wrong."}, {"label": "The objective", "text": "Minimize total absolute forecast flow remaining after the transfers. The bike budget and station-touch limit are scenario inputs. Equal scores prefer fewer moves, then fewer stations."}, {"label": "The constraints", "text": "Transfers must balance across the network. Total bikes moved and stations changed stay within the selected limits. Protected and nonadmitted stations receive no transfer. Stock, dock and vehicle constraints are not represented."}]}>
 <p class="model-control-explanation"><strong>A planning envelope.</strong> The data contain journeys, not historical bike stocks, dock availability or truck routes. The controls explore where effort could be concentrated; they cannot establish that the transfers could actually be dispatched.</p>
 <details class="data-details model-formulation"><summary>Variables, constraints and comparison policies</summary>
  <p>For station <var>s</var>, net outflow <var>d</var><sub>s</sub> = morning departures − arrivals. The decision <var>r</var><sub>s</sub> is an integer net transfer into the station: positive adds bikes; negative removes them.</p>
  <ul class="model-equations"><li>Minimize ∑ |forecast <var>d</var><sub>s</sub> − <var>r</var><sub>s</sub>|</li><li>∑ <var>r</var><sub>s</sub> = 0</li><li>½ ∑ |<var>r</var><sub>s</sub>| ≤ bike budget <var>B</var></li><li>Number of nonzero transfers ≤ station limit <var>K</var></li><li>Protected and nonadmitted stations have zero transfer.</li></ul>
  <p>The exact integer solver enumerates station-pool sizes and combines marginal improvements. Its result is independently checked against exhaustive cases and a general integer-programming model. The greedy rule chooses the best immediate feasible pair; it may tie the exact result.</p>
  <p>No relocation provides the starting score. A perfect-information reference uses the observed morning flows in advance, under the same restrictions. It is unavailable when planning. Forecast means retain their fractional precision; only bike transfers are whole numbers.</p>
  <p>After the morning, the score becomes ∑ |observed <var>d</var><sub>s</sub> − planned <var>r</var><sub>s</sub>| across all ordinary stations. This is residual observed flow imbalance under a hypothetical transfer, not measured shortages, unmet trips or service improvement. Journeys crossing the window can leave a nonzero network total; transfers conserve that total.</p>
 </details>
</DecisionBrief>
